1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teod%C3%B3sio_II,_Duke_of_Braganza
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00027933&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Teodósio II of Bragança (Portuguese pronunciation: [tiuˈdɔziu]), (1568, Vila Viçosa - 1630) was the 7th Duke of Braganza. He was the son of John I, 6th Duke of Bragança (1543-1583) by his wife, Infanta (Princess) Catherine of Guim
2 CONC arães (1540-1614).
2 CONT
2 CONT As a child, Teodósio was brought to the court and made page to the king Sebastian I of Portugal. The king was very fond of him and in 1578 insisted on taking him to Africa in the expedition against the king of Morocco. This milita
2 CONC ry campaign was doomed. During the ill-fated battle of Alcacer-Quibir, Teodósio remained at the side of his king until the situation become extremely dangerous. Then, Sebastian ordered servants to take the ten-year-old child to sa
2 CONC fety behind the lines. The young man was not happy to be set aside and ran away at the first opportunity. Teodósio mounted a horse and went to the front lines of the battle, pursued by very scared servants. Eventually, like many o
2 CONC thers, he was wounded and taken prisoner. Back in Portugal, his father João went mad with grief and offered a fortune for the ransom of his heir. He also asked king Philip II of Spain to write to the king of Morocco in order to sp
2 CONC are young Teodósio's life. There was no need for such alarm. Impressed by the tale of his courage in battle, the king of Morocco let the child go, safely and without ransom, in August 1579.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile in Portugal, the situation was one of unrest. With the disappearance of King Sebastian in the battle, the new king was Cardinal Henry I of Portugal an old childless man. Teodósio was the son of Infanta Catarina, an ambit
2 CONC ious woman who, with her husband the Duke of Bragança, aspired to the throne. Philip II of Spain also aspired to the Portuguese throne and used every means to keep young Teodósio (great-grandson of King Manuel I and a possible thr
2 CONC eat) out of the country. Only when he had safely secured the crown did Philip II of Spain, now Philip I of Portugal, allow the child's return.
2 CONT
2 CONT Teodósio became a duke in 1583 at his father's death and grew up to be a faithful servant of the Spanish king of Portugal. He defended Lisbon from the attacks of another candidate (António of Crato) and was responsible for the kin
2 CONC gdom's security for many years. Acknowledging this fidelity, Philip II granted the Braganças more lands and titles.
2 CONT
