1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaspar_T%C3%A9llez-Gir%C3%B3n,_5th_Duke_de_Osuna
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaspar Téllez-Girón, 5th Duke de Osuna (25 May 1625 – Madrid, 2 June 1694), 5th Marquess of Peñafiel, 9th Count of Ureña and other lesser titles, was a Spanish general and a Grandee of Spain, title bestowed by king Philip II of Sp
2 CONC ain on 5 February 1562 when Pedro Téllez-Girón y de la Cueva, 5th Count of Ureña, a.k.a. Pedro Girón de la Cueva, Viceroy of Naples, was given the title of 1st Duke of Osuna. Osuna, a very old town at the province of Seville, was 
2 CONC already an indigenous Iberian civilization town occupied by the Romans well over 2,000 years ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Dukes of Osuna, in general, have excelled as Viceroys and/or Governors in European territories linked or broadly allied with Imperial Spain for several centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT He lost the Battle of Castelo Rodrigo in 1664, as part of the Portuguese Restoration War. He was Viceroy of Catalonia, 1667–1669, Governor of the Duchy of Milan, Italy, (1670–1674), when he was replaced by Claude Lamoral, Prince o
2 CONC f Ligne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family ancestors background
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the grandson of Pedro Téllez-Girón, 3rd Duke of Osuna, Viceroy of Sicily, Viceroy of Naples, and the son of Juan Téllez-Girón, 4rd Duke of Osuna, Viceroy of Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Gaspar marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT In his firs marriage in 1645 he married a cousin, named as her mother Felice, Feliche de Sandoval Rojas y Enriquez de Cabrera, deceased in Milan, Italy on 7 October 1671, who hold her own inherited title of 4th Duchess of Uceda an
2 CONC d was therefore, also a Grandee of Spain on her own rights, daughter of Francisco de Sandoval Rojas, 2nd Duke of Lerma and 2nd Duke of Uceda, Duke of Cea. Her mother was a member of the powerful and old Colonna family. The childre
2 CONC n who survived, (5 out of 7), were all females who married within the pinnacle of the highest Spanish Nobility of the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married again in 1672, aged 47, 19 years old Ana Antonia Francisca, (Milán, 14 April 1653 - Madrid, 4 December 1707, aged 50), 4th Marchioness of Caracena, a daughter of Luis de Benavides Carrillo, Marquis of Caracena, 6th marq
2 CONC uis of Frómista, Governor of the Duchy of Milan, Governor of the Habsburg Netherlands. Her mother was Catalina Ponce de León, third daughter of Rodrigo Ponce de León, 4th Duke of Arcos and of María Francica Córdoba de Aragón, a da
2 CONC ughter of the 5th Dukes of Segorbe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ana Antonia Francisca, would be the inheritor of the nobility titles of her parents, presumably by the non existence of/or earlier death of her legitimate male brothers. She held also the title of Marshal of Castile by herself.
2 CONT
2 CONT From this second wife (4 children:2 males + 2 females), came the two next successors to the Duchy,
2 CONT  Francisco Téllez-Girón, 6th Duke of Osuna, a.k.a. Francisco María de Paula Téllez-Girón y Benavides, (* Madrid, Spain, 11 August 1678 - Ambassador and Plenipotentiary minister for the Treaty of Utrecht, (1713) - París, France, 13
2 CONC  April 1716). Married in 1695 he got two daughters only who did not inherited the title passing it to the surviving brother:
2 CONT
2 CONT  José María Téllez-Girón, 7th Duke of Osuna, a.k.a. José María Téllez-Girón y Benavides, (* Madrid, Spain, 25 May 1685 - Duke successor to his brother 1716 - + Madrid 8 March 1733). He married in 1721, Andalusian wealthy girl Fran
2 CONC cisca Bibiana, 6th daughter of the very rich and powerful 12th Duke of Medina Sidonia, 17th Count of Niebla, 11th Marquess of Cazaza, a Grandee of Spain, a Knight of the Order of the Golden Fleece. Her mother, Luisa María Bernarda
2 CONC , was the third daughter of the financier and nobleman 5th Duke of Pastrana, of the "De Silva Mendoza" family. With Issue.
