1 OCCU Queen Consort of England
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_of_Modena
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary of Modena (Italian: Maria Beatrice Eleonora Anna Margherita Isabella d'Este;[1] 5 October [O.S. 25 September] 1658 – 7 May [O.S. 26 April] 1718) was Queen of England, Scotland and Ireland as the second wife of James VII and I
2 CONC I. A devout Roman Catholic, Mary married the widower James, who was then the younger brother and heir presumptive of Charles II.[2] She was devoted to James and their children, two of whom survived to adulthood: the Jacobite claim
2 CONC ant to the thrones, James Francis Edward Stuart, and Louisa Maria Stuart.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Born a princess of the northwestern Italian Duchy of Modena, Mary is primarily remembered for the controversial birth of Prince James Francis Edward, her only surviving son. It was widely rumored that he was smuggled into the birt
2 CONC h chamber in a warming pan in order to perpetuate her husband's Roman Catholic Stuart dynasty. James Francis Edward's birth was a contributing factor to the "Glorious Revolution", the revolution which deposed James II and VII, and
2 CONC  replaced him with Mary II, a Protestant, the elder of James II's daughters from his first marriage to Anne Hyde (1637–1671). Mary II and her husband, William III of Orange, would reign jointly over all three kingdoms.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary went into exile in France, being known as the "Queen over the Water" among the Jacobites. She lived with her husband and children in the Château de Saint-Germain-en-Laye, provided by King Louis XIV. Mary was popular among Lou
2 CONC is's courtiers; James, however, was considered a bore. In widowhood, Mary spent time with the nuns at the Convent of Chaillot, frequently during summers with her daughter, Louisa Maria Teresa. In 1701, when James II died, young Ja
2 CONC mes Francis Edward, aged 13, became king in the eyes of the Jacobites. Given that he was too young to assume the nominal reins of government, Mary represented him until he reached the age of 16. When James Francis Edward was asked
2 CONC  to leave France as part of the settlement from the Treaty of Utrecht in 1713, which ended the War of the Spanish Succession (1701–1714), Mary stayed despite having no family there, her daughter having died of smallpox. Fondly rem
2 CONC embered by her French contemporaries, Mary died of breast cancer in 1718.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life (1658–1673)
2 CONT Formal portrait of Mary's father as a young man. He has long bushy hair and a fleshy face and wears a black suit of armor with a brown shoulder sash.
2 CONT Alfonso IV d'Este, Duke of Modena, Mary's father, in a portrait by Justus Sustermans
2 CONT Maria Beatrice d'Este, the second (but eldest surviving) child of Alfonso IV d'Este, Duke of Modena, and his wife, Laura Martinozzi, was born on 5 October 1658 NS[note 1] in Modena, Duchy of Modena, Italy. Her only younger brother
2 CONC , Francesco, succeeded their father as Duke upon the latter's death in 1662, the year Mary turned four.[4] Mary and Francesco's mother, Laura, was strict with them and acted as regent of the duchy until her son came of age.[5][6] 
2 CONC Mary's education was excellent;[7] she spoke Italian and French fluently, had a good knowledge of Latin and, later, mastered English.[8][9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary was described by contemporaries as "tall and admirably shaped", and was sought as a bride for James, Duke of York, by Lord Peterborough.[10][11] Lord Peterborough was Groom of the Stool to the Duke of York. A widower, James w
2 CONC as the younger brother and heir of Charles II of England.[12] Duchess Laura was not initially forthcoming with a reply to Peterborough's proposal, hoping, according to the French ambassador, for a "grander" match with the eleven-y
2 CONC ear-old Charles II of Spain.[13][14] Whatever the reason for Laura's initial reluctance, she finally accepted the proposal on behalf of Mary, and they were married by proxy on 30 September 1673, she just shy of 15, he being 39.[15
2 CONC ]
2 CONT
2 CONT Modena was within the sphere of influence of Louis XIV of France, who endorsed Mary's candidature and greeted Mary warmly in Paris, where she stopped en route to England, giving her a brooch worth £8,000.[16][note 2] Her reception
2 CONC  in England was much cooler.[18] Parliament, which was entirely composed of Protestants, reacted poorly to the news of a Roman Catholic marriage, fearing it was a "Papist" plot against the country.[18] The English public, who were
2 CONC  predominantly Protestant, branded the Duchess of York – as Mary was thereafter known as until her husband's accession – the "Pope's daughter".[19] Parliament threatened to have the marriage annulled,[19] leading Charles to suspen
2 CONC d parliament until 7 January 1674 OS, to ensure the marriage would be honoured and safeguarding the reputation of his House of Stuart.[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Duchess of York (1673–1685)
2 CONT Household
2 CONT Formal three-quarter length portrait of James aged about thirty. He has a long face with large cleft chin and red lips. He has long blonde hair and poses in black armor, with a brocade sash and lace cravate and clasped a baton in 
2 CONC his right-hand.
2 CONT James, Duke of York, in a portrait by Sir Peter Lely
2 CONT James, an avowed Roman Catholic, was twenty-five years older than his bride, scarred by smallpox and afflicted with a stammer.[20] He had secretly converted to Catholicism around 1668.[21] Mary first saw her husband on 23 November
2 CONC  1673 OS, on the day of their second marriage ceremony.[22][23] James was pleased with his bride.[24] Mary, however, at first disliked him, and burst into tears each time she saw him.[25] Nonetheless, she soon warmed to James.[3] 
2 CONC From his first marriage to Anne Hyde, a commoner, who had died in 1671, James had two daughters: Lady Mary and Lady Anne.[26] They were introduced to Mary by James with the words, "I have brought you a new play-fellow".[26] Unlike
2 CONC  Lady Mary, Lady Anne disliked her father's new wife.[7] Mary played games with Anne to win her affection.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Duchess of York received £5,000 spending money annually and her own household, headed by Carey Fraser, Countess of Peterborough; it was frequented by ladies of her husband's selection: Frances Stewart, Duchess of Richmond and 
2 CONC Anne Scott, 1st Duchess of Buccleuch.[12][27][28][29] That Mary loathed gambling did not stop her ladies compelling her to do so almost every day.[30] They believed that "if she refrained, it might be taken ill".[30] Consequently,
2 CONC  Mary incurred minor gambling debts.[30]
2 CONT
2 CONT The birth of Mary's first child, Catherine Laura, named after Queen Catherine, on 10 January 1675 OS represented the beginning of a string of children that would die in infancy.[31] At this time the Duchess of York was on excellen
2 CONC t terms with Lady Mary, and visited her in The Hague after the younger Mary had married William of Orange. She travelled incognito and took Lady Anne with her.[32]
2 CONT
2 CONT Popish Plot and exile
2 CONT Main article: Popish Plot
2 CONT The Duchess's Catholic secretary, Edward Colman, was, in 1678, falsely implicated in a fictitious plot against the King by Titus Oates.[33] The plot, known as the Popish Plot, led to the Exclusionist movement, which was headed by 
2 CONC Anthony Ashley Cooper, 1st Earl of Shaftesbury.[34] The Exclusionists sought to debar the Catholic Duke of York from the throne.[35] Their reputation in tatters, the Yorks were reluctantly exiled to Brussels, a domain of King Char
2 CONC les II of Spain, ostensibly to visit Lady Mary—since 1677 the wife of Prince William III of Orange.[36][37][38] Accompanied by her not yet three-year-old daughter Isabella and Lady Anne, the Duchess of York was saddened by James's
2 CONC  extra-marital affair with Catherine Sedley.[39] Mary's spirits were briefly revived by a visit from her mother, who was living in Rome.[40]
2 CONT
2 CONT An informal portrait of Mary. She has a long handsome face, dark eyes and black hair. Her hair, her brown satin dress and plain linen undergarment are in fashionable disarray. She clasps a white dog.
2 CONT Mary in the year of her husband's accession, 1685, in a painting by the Dutch artist Willem Wissing
2 CONT A report that King Charles was very sick sent the Yorks hastily back to England.[41] They feared the King's eldest illegitimate son, James Scott, 1st Duke of Monmouth, the commander of England's armed forces, might usurp the crown
2 CONC  if Charles died before their return.[41][42] The danger was compounded by the fact that Monmouth enjoyed the support of the Exclusionists, who held a majority in the English House of Commons.[41] Charles survived but, feeling the
2 CONC  Yorks returned to court too soon, sent James and Mary to Edinburgh, where they stayed on-and-off for the next three years.[43][44] Lodging in Holyrood Palace, the Yorks had to make do without Ladies Anne and Isabella, who stayed 
2 CONC in London on Charles's orders.[45] The Yorks were recalled to London in February 1680, only to return again to Edinburgh that autumn; this time they went on a more honorable footing: James was created King's Commissioner to Scotla
2 CONC nd.[46] Separated from Lady Isabella once again, Mary sank into a state of sadness, exacerbated by the passing of the Exclusion bill in the Commons.[47][48] Isabella, thus far the only one of Mary's children to survive infancy, di
2 CONC ed in February 1681.[49] Isabella's death plunged Mary into a religious mania, worrying her physician.[49] At the same time as news reached Holyrood of Isabella's death, Mary's mother was falsely accused of offering £10,000 for th
2 CONC e murder of the King.[49] The accuser, a pamphleteer, was executed by order of the King.[49]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Exclusionist reaction that followed the Popish Plot had died down by May 1682.[50] Exclusionist-dominated Parliament, suspended since March 1681, never again met in the reign of Charles II.[51] Therefore, the Duke and Duchess 
2 CONC returned to England, and Mary gave birth to a daughter named Charlotte Mary in August 1682; Charlotte Mary's death three weeks later, according to the French ambassador, robbed James of "hope that any child of his can live"—all Ja
2 CONC mes's sons by his first wife died in infancy.[52] James's sadness was dispelled by his revival in popularity following the discovery of a plot to kill him and Charles.[53] The objective of the plot, known as the Rye House Plot, wa
2 CONC s to have Monmouth placed on the throne as Lord Protector.[54] The revival was so strong that, in 1684, James was re-admitted to the Privy Council, after an absence of eleven years.[55]
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen consort (1685–1688)
2 CONT Despite all the furor over Exclusionism, James ascended his brother's thrones easily upon the latter's death – which occurred on 6 February 1685 OS – possibly owing to the risk that the said alternative might provoke another civil
2 CONC  war.[56] Mary sincerely mourned Charles, recalling in later life, "He was always kind to me."[57] Mary and James's £119,000 coronation, occurring on 23 April OS, Saint George's day, was meticulously planned.[58][59] Precedents we
2 CONC re sought for Mary because a full-length joint coronation had not occurred since the coronation of King Henry VIII and Queen Catherine.[58]
2 CONT
2 CONT An informal portrait of Mary with her infant son. She is seated, wearing a regal velvet cloak edged with ermine. The boy, aged about one year, stands on a table and is held by his mother. He wears a cream satin dress with lace bon
2 CONC net, sleeves and apron.
2 CONT Queen Mary with her son, James Francis Edward, by Benedetto Gennari the Younger
2 CONT Queen Mary's health had still not recovered after the death of Lady Isabella. So much so, in fact, that the Tuscan envoy reported to Florence that "general opinion turns [for Mary's successor] in the direction of the Princess, You
2 CONC r Highness's daughter".[60][61] France, too, was preparing for Mary's imminent demise, putting forward as its candidate for James's new wife the Duke of Enghien's daughter.[60] The Queen was then trying to make her brother, the Du
2 CONC ke of Modena, marry the former, Anna Maria Luisa de' Medici.[62]
2 CONT
2 CONT In February 1687, Mary, at the time irritated by James's affair with Catherine Sedley, Countess of Dorchester, moved into new apartments in Whitehall; Whitehall had been home to a Catholic chapel since December 1686.[63][64] Her a
2 CONC partments were designed by Christopher Wren at the cost of £13,000.[65] Because the palace's renovation was thus far unfinished, James received ambassadors in her rooms, much to Mary's chagrin.[66] Five months later, shortly after
2 CONC  the marriage talks with Tuscany collapsed, the Queen's mother, Duchess Laura, died.[67] Therefore, the whole English court went into mourning.[67] Duchess Laura left Mary "a considerable sum of cash" and some jewelry.[68] William
2 CONC  III of Orange, James's nephew and son-in-law, sensed popular discontent with James's government; he used the death of Mary's mother as a guise to send his cousin Count Zuylestein, to England, ostensibly to condole the Queen, but 
2 CONC in reality as a spy.[69][70]
2 CONT
2 CONT Formal seated portrait of Mary II. She wears a grey satin décolletage dress and a blue satin cloak with gold swathes at her shoulders. Her hair is formally arranged in curls, and she wears a necklace of large grey pearls.
2 CONT Mary II of England in a painting by Sir Peter Lely
2 CONT Having visited Bath, in the hope its waters would aid conception, Queen Mary became pregnant in late 1687.[71] When the pregnancy became public knowledge shortly before Christmas, Catholics rejoiced.[72] Protestants, who had toler
2 CONC ated James's Catholic government because he had no Catholic heir, were concerned.[73] The Protestant disillusion came to a head after the child was known to be male, and many Protestants believed the child was spurious;[74] if not
2 CONC , James II's Catholic dynasty would have been perpetuated.[74] Popular opinion alleged that the child, named James Francis Edward Stuart, was smuggled into the birth chamber as a substitute for the Queen's real but stillborn child
2 CONC .[74] This rumor was widely accepted as fact by Protestants, despite the many witnesses of the birth. [74][75] Mainly by mismanagement on James's part, these rumors had some excuse as from personal prejudice he had excluded many f
2 CONC rom the ceremony whose testimony must have been counted valid; most of the witnesses were Catholics or foreigners, and several, such as his daughter Anne and the Protestant prelates, or the maternal relatives of his daughters, who
2 CONC m the new birth would remove from the direct succession, were not present.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne and her elder sister, Mary, still suspected that their father had thrust a changeling upon the nation.[69] Count Zuylestein, returning to the Netherlands shortly after the birth, agreed with Anne's findings.[69]
2 CONT
2 CONT Issued by seven leading Whig nobles, the invitation for William to invade England signaled the beginning of a revolution that culminated in James's deposition. [76] The invitation assured William that "nineteen parts of twenty of 
2 CONC the people throughout the kingdom" wished for an intervention. [76] The revolution, known as the Glorious Revolution, deprived James Francis Edward of his right to the English throne, on the grounds that he was not deemed the King
2 CONC 's real son, and later because he was a Catholic.[76] With England in the hands of William of Orange's 15,000-strong army, James and Mary decided to go into exile in France.[76] On 9 December 1688, Mary left London in disguise wit
2 CONC h the infant Prince of Wales and in the company of Victoria Davia-Montecuculi, under the arrangement of Antoine Nompar de Caumont. After arriving in France through Calais, she was joined by James a few weeks later. There, they sta
2 CONC yed at the expense of James's first cousin King Louis XIV, who supported the Jacobite cause.[76][77]
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen over the water (1688–1701)
2 CONT Reception at Louis XIV's court
2 CONT Formal full-length portrait of Louis XIV wearing long curling black wig and state robes including a cloak embroidered with golden fleur de lys.
2 CONT Louis XIV in a portrait by Hyacinthe Rigaud, 1701[77]
2 CONT James was formally deposed on 11 December 1688 OS in England and on 11 May 1689 OS in Scotland, and his daughter Mary II and her husband, William III, were made joint monarchs.[78] James, however, backed by Louis XIV of France, st
2 CONC ill considered himself king by divine right, and maintained it was not within parliament's prerogative to depose a monarch.[79] Louis gave the exiled royal couple the use of Château de Saint-Germain-en-Laye, where they set up a co
2 CONC urt-in-exile.[77][80]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary quickly became a popular fixture at Louis XIV's court at Versailles, where diarist Madame de Sévigné acclaimed Mary for her "distinguished bearing and her quick wit".[81] Questions of precedence, however, marred Mary's relati
2 CONC ons with Louis's daughter-in-law, Maria Anna Victoria of Bavaria.[81] Because Mary was accorded the privileges and rank of a queen, Maria Anna was outranked by her.[81] Therefore, Maria Anna refused to see Mary, etiquette being a 
2 CONC sensitive issue at Versailles.[82] In spite of this, Louis and his secret wife, Madame de Maintenon, became close friends with Mary.[81] As there was no queen at the French court, nor a dauphine after Maria Anna's death in 1690, M
2 CONC ary took precedence over all the female members of the French court and French royal house, as did her daughter in her capacity of a royal princess until the next French dauphine appeared in 1711.[83] James was largely excluded fr
2 CONC om French court life. His contemporaries found him boring, and French courtiers frequently joked that "when one talks to him, one understands why he is here."[81][84] Mary gave birth to a daughter, Louise Mary, in 1692.[81] She wa
2 CONC s to be James and Mary's last child.
2 CONT
2 CONT Initially supported by Irish Catholics in his effort to regain the thrones, James launched an expedition to Ireland in March 1689.[85] He abandoned it upon his defeat at the Battle of the Boyne in 1690.[85] During James's campaign
2 CONC , Mary supported his cause throughout the British Isles: she sent three French supply ships to Bantry Bay and £2,000 to Jacobite rebels in Dundee.[86] She financed those measures by selling her jewelry.[87] Money problems plagued 
2 CONC the Stuart court-in-exile, despite a substantial pension from Louis XIV of 50,000 livres.[77] Mary tried her best to assist those of her husband's followers living in poverty, and encouraged her children to give part of their pock
2 CONC et money to Jacobite refugees.[88][89][90]
2 CONT
2 CONT Estensi succession
2 CONT The collapse of James's invasion of Ireland in 1691 upsets Mary. Her spirits were lifted by news of the marriage of her brother, the Duke of Modena, to Margherita Maria Farnese of Parma.[91] When, in 1695, Mary's brother died, the
2 CONC  House of Este was left with one progenitor, their uncle Cardinal-Duke Rinaldo.[92] Queen Mary, concerned for the dynasty's future, urged the Cardinal-Duke to resign his cardinalate, "for the good of the people and for the perpetu
2 CONC ation of the sovereign house of Este".[93] Rinaldo's bride, Princess Charlotte Felicitas of Brunswick-Lüneburg, was, according to Mary, "of an easy disposition best suited to [the Duke]".[93]
2 CONT
2 CONT A bone of contention, however, arose over the Queen's inheritance and dowry.[94] Duke Rinaldo refused to release the former, and left the latter £15,000 in arrears.[95] In 1700, five years later, Rinaldo finally paid Mary her dowr
2 CONC y; her inheritance, however, remained sequestered, and relations with Modena worsened again when Rinaldo allied himself with Holy Roman Emperor Leopold I.[96] Leopold was an enemy of Louis XIV.[96]
2 CONT
2 CONT Regency (1701–1704)
2 CONT A heraldic shield emblazoned with the emblems of France, Scotland, England, Ireland and the House of Este.
2 CONT Mary's coat of arms as Queen of England.[97] Depicting the Royal Coat of Arms of England, Scotland and Ireland impaled with a minor version of her father's arms as Duke of Modena. In light of religious sentiment at the time, it wa
2 CONC s presumed unwise to reproduce her father's arms in full, since the quarterings are divided by a "Pale Gules charged with the Papal keys ensigned with the Tiara".[98]
2 CONT In March 1701, James suffered a stroke while hearing mass at the Château de Saint-Germain-en-Laye, leaving him partially paralyzed.[99] Fagon, Louis XIV's personal physician, recommended the waters of Bourbon-l'Archambault, to cur
2 CONC e James's paralysis.[100] The waters, however, had little effect, and James died of a seizure on 16 September 1701.[101] Louis, contravening the Peace of Ryswick and irritating William, declared James Francis Edward King of Englan
2 CONC d, Ireland and Scotland as James III and VIII.[102] Mary acted as nominal regent for her minor son.[103] She presided over his regency council, too, although she was uninterested in politics.[103] Before his death, James II expres
2 CONC sed his wish that Mary's regency would last no longer than their son's 18th birthday.[104]
2 CONT
2 CONT A young man wears a powdered wig while posing in a suit of armor.
2 CONT James Francis Edward Stuart, Mary's only surviving son, in a portrait by Antonio David.[95]
2 CONT Dressed in mourning for the remainder of her life, Mary's first act as regent was to disseminate a manifesto, outlining James Francis Edward's claims.[105] It was largely ignored in England.[105] In Scotland, however, the confeder
2 CONC ate Lords sent Lord Belhaven to Saint-Germain, to convince the Queen to surrender to them custody of James Francis Edward and accede to his conversion to Protestantism.[105] The conversion, said Belhaven, would enable his accessio
2 CONC n to the English throne upon William's death.[106] Mary was not swayed by Belhaven's argument, so a compromise was reached: James Francis Edward, if he became king, would limit the number of Catholic priests in England and promise
2 CONC  not to tamper with the established Church of England.[106] In exchange, the confederate Lords would do all in their power to block the passing of the Hanoverian succession in Scottish parliament.[106] When, in March 1702, William
2 CONC  died, Lord Lovat declared for James Francis Edward at Inverness.[107] Soon after, Lovat travelled to the court-in-exile at Saint-Germain, and begged Mary to allow her son to come to Scotland.[107] Lovat intended to raise an army 
2 CONC of 15,000 soldiers in Scotland to seize the throne for James Francis Edward.[107] Mary refused to part with James Francis Edward, and the rising failed.[107] Mary's regency ceased with her son's reaching of the age of 16.[108]
2 CONT
2 CONT Later life
2 CONT Having wished to become a nun in her youth, Mary sought refuge from the stresses of exile at the Convent of the Visitandines, Chaillot, near Paris, where she befriended Louis's penitent mistress, Louise de La Vallière.[109] There,
2 CONC  Mary stayed with her daughter for long periods almost every summer.[110] It was here, too, in 1711, that Mary found out that, as part of the embryonic Treaty of Utrecht, James Francis Edward was to lose Louis's explicit recogniti
2 CONC on and be forced to leave France.[110] The next year, when James Francis Edward was expelled and Louise Mary died of smallpox, Mary was very upset;[111] according to Mary's close friend Madame de Maintenon, Mary was "a model of de
2 CONC solation".[111] Deprived of the company of her family, Mary lived out the rest of her days at Chaillot and Saint-Germain in virtual poverty, unable to travel by her own means because all her horses had died and she could not affor
2 CONC d to replace them.[112]
2 CONT
2 CONT Following her death from cancer on 7 May 1718, Mary was remembered fondly by her French contemporaries, three of whom, Elizabeth Charlotte of the Palatinate, the Duke of Saint-Simon and the Marquis of Dangeau, deemed her a "saint"
2 CONC .[113][114] Mary's remains were interred in Chaillot among the nuns she had befriended.[115]
2 CONT
