2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Parma
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth Farnese
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth was born at the Palazzo della Pilotta in Parma, the capital of a duchy which had been ruled by her family for over two centuries. Elisabeth would later become the heiress of her father's dominions after her uncle Frances
2 CONC co Farnese, Duke of Parma and his younger brother, both of whom remained childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Duchy of Parma would later be inherited by her first son Infante Carlos, and after his accession to the Spanish throne the title passed on to her third son Infante Felipe. It was he who founded the modern day House of Bourbon-
2 CONC Parma.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother educated her in strict seclusion, but even this measure failed to tame her imperious and ambitious temper. At the age of twenty-one (24 December 1714) she was married by proxy at Parma to Philip V of Spain. The marriage
2 CONC  was arranged by Cardinal Alberoni, with the concurrence of the Princesse des Ursins, the Camarera Mayor of the King of Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Spain
2 CONT
2 CONT On arriving at the Spanish border, Elizabeth was met by the Princesse des Ursins, but received her sternly, and, perhaps in accordance with a plan previously concerted with the king, at once ordered her removal from her presence a
2 CONC nd from Spain. Elizabeth quickly obtained complete influence over Philip V, who was considered by all means a weak king. This influence was exerted altogether in support of Alberoni's policy, one chief aim of which was to recover 
2 CONC the ancient Italian possessions of Spain, and which actually resulted in the seizure of Sardinia and Sicily. So vigorously did she enter into this policy that, when the French forces advanced to the Pyrenees, she placed herself at
2 CONC  the head of one division of the Spanish army.
2 CONT
2 CONT But Elizabeth's ambition was grievously disappointed. The Triple Alliance thwarted her plans with British troops raiding Vigo, and at length in 1720 the allies made the banishment of Alberoni a condition of peace. Sicily also had 
2 CONC to be evacuated and finally all her entreaties failed to prevent the abdication of Philip, who in 1724 gave up the throne in favour of his firstborn heir (from his first marriage), and retired to the palace of La Granja. (Also in 
2 CONC 1724, she acquired the San Ildefonso Group for him from the Odescalchi family.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip V and Elisabeth in 1739
2 CONT
2 CONT Seven months later, however, the death of the young king recalled Philip to the throne. During his later years, when he was nearly senile, she directed the whole policy of Spain so as to secure thrones in Italy for her sons. In 17
2 CONC 31 she had the satisfaction of seeing her favorite scheme realized with the recognition by the powers in the Treaty of Vienna of her son Don Carlos (afterwards Charles III of Spain) as the Duke of Parma, and after the Treaty of Vi
2 CONC enna (1738) his accession to the throne of the Two Sicilies. Her second son, Philip, became Duke of Parma in 1748.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Elisabeth's time, Spain was a leading power in the world with a large empire stretching from its hub in Europe to much of the Americas.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Widowhood
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth survived her husband by twenty years. In the time between his death in 1746 and her own in 1766, she witnessed many events: the accession to the Spanish throne of her stepson, Ferdinand VI and Barbara of Portugal, whom s
2 CONC he hated; and the accession to the throne of Parma of her beloved second son, Philip. In 1752 she built Riofrio Palace as her dowager residence. One year before her death she had the satisfaction of seeing the marriage between her
2 CONC  grandson, Charles, Prince of Asturias (future Charles IV), and her granddaugther Maria Luisa of Parma.
2 CONT
2 CONT She later spent much of her time at the palaces of La Granja and Aranjuez. It was there that she died in 1766 at the age of 73. She was buried next to her husband in the Colegiata of San Ildefonso.
2 CONT
