1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mariana de Silva-Bazán y Sarmiento''', also known as Mariana de Silva-Meneses and Sarmiento de Sotomayor, (Madrid, 14 October 1739 - Madrid, 17 January 1784), was an 18th-century Spanish aristocrat, writer, painter, and transla
2 CONC tor.[1] She is remembered for being a curious example of ambidexterity, being able to write and paint with both hands. She was an academic at the Real Academia de Bellas Artes de San Fernando, serving also as honorary director.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mariana wrote poetic works and translated some tragedies from French, also making paintings of merit according to her panegyrists, although neither her literary nor her pictorial works have been preserved to prove it. On 20 July 1
2 CONC 766, she was named an academic at the Real Academia de Bellas Artes de San Fernando in Madrid, of which she became honorary director.[1][3][4] She was also a member of the Imperial Academy of Arts of Saint Petersburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mariana_de_Silva-Bazán_y_Sarmiento
2 CONT
