1 CONT abdicate, he died in Rome in 1808.
0 @N6000000002188434264@ NOTE Carlos IV, King of Spain from 1788 to 1808, second son of King Carlos III, whom he succeeded in place of his imbecile older brother. Unlike his father, Carlos IV was an ineffective ruler and in 1792 virtually surrendered the gover
1 CONC nment to Godoy, his chief minister and the favorite of his wife, María Luisa. Spain entered the French Revolutionary Wars in 1793, but in 1795 made peace with France in the second Treaty of Basel. By the Treaty of San Ildefonso in
1 CONC  1796, Spain allied itself with France and became involved in the war with England. It suffered major naval defeats at Cape St. Vincent in 1797 and Trafalgar in 1805. The convention of Fontainebleau in1807 precipitated the events 
1 CONC leading to the Peninsular War. As French troops marched on Madrid in Mar., 1808, a popular uprising led to a coup at Aranjuez; the King was forced to abdicate in favor of his son, King Ferdinand VII. Napoleon I tricked both father
1 CONC  and son into a meeting with him at Bayonne, France, and forced them to abdicate in turn. The royal family was held captive in France until 1814, while Joseph Bonaparte was king of Spain. Charles IV and his family have been frankl
1 CONC y portrayed by Goya, one of their court painters.
0 @N6000000002240840273@ NOTE His wife Maria had complete influence on him, causing a vascillating policy.
1 CONT There were long periods of trouble with Revolutionary France, subservience to
1 CONT Napoleon, war with Portugal and England, his Spanish fleet was destroyed at
1 CONT Trafalgar (1805) and Spain was invaded by French armies(1807).  Forced to
1 CONT abdicate, he died in Rome in 1808.
0 @N6000000002244433088@ NOTE His wife Maria had complete influence on him, causing a vascillating policy.
1 CONT There were long periods of trouble with Revolutionary France, subservience to
1 CONT Napoleon, war with Portugal and England, his Spanish fleet was destroyed at
1 CONT Trafalgar (1805) and Spain was invaded by French armies(1807).  Forced to
1 CONT abdicate, he died in Rome in 1808.
0 @N6000000002322303708@ NOTE A relatively weak and inept ruler, he was greatly influenced by his wife
1 CONT in the kingdom's fight against the French Revolution and its liberal ideas.
1 CONT In 1806, Napoleon's army captured Naples, forcing Ferdinand's flight to
1 CONT Sicily, where he removed Maria Carolina from the court and appointed son
1 CONT Francis as regent, granting the Sicilians a constitution. With the fall of
1 CONT Napoleon, Ferdinand returned to Naples as Ferdinand I of the united kingdom
1 CONT of Two Sicilies (12/1816), overthrowing the Naples constitution in 3/1821.
